"gnohmon@panix.com" <gnohmon@panix.com> wrote:

> Another problem is that you are increasing the
> probability of getting a bones file above the
> "natural" probability.

On the other hand, the choice to just delete all
bones files so that each game is strictly
self-standing, works in the opposite direction, and
no one seems to find that objectionable.

Also, since probably most of us play NetHack these
days on our own computers, there is no "natural"
probability until the game reaches some "steady
state", which, if your skills improve with playing
experience, it will never do. You start with no
bones files, over time some accumulate, and in the
ideal, you eventually ascend every game and all the
bones files are depleted as they are encountered.

> Why people think it's okay to do this with hearse
> but not okay to do it with your own boneses,
> that's a mystery.

With Hearse, that's purely a run time decision by
the user. Hearse will either _replace_ your own
bones file, if you pass the appropriate flag, or
_reward_ you for your own bones file with an
additional file (the default behavior).

I'd probably slightly rather the default were the
other way, but I have no problem with an ever
increasing supply of bones files on one's personal
NetHack installation, either.

I don't know _how_ it is accomplished in a
multi-player installation of NetHack, but I'm
guessing that it is, that more than just one bones
file per level can exist, so you are bringing the
individual installation into slow parity to a large
multi-player installation.

Even I'm wrong, and  only one bones file can exist
per level, on a large installation either, most
levels would have bones files "most of the time",
I'd think, though that might depend on whether the
chance of dropping a bones file equals the chance of
picking up an existing bones file.

HTH

